UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 33, Lesson 2: Cultural exchange', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate. Analyse published examples of exchange (food, language learning, arts collaborations) and check for stereotypes. First Nations cultural materials require protocols—use only approved sources; do not treat culture as costume.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nGive one respectful exchange example and one stereotype to avoid, with a brief why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Respectful example credits people; stereotype named without repeating harmful detail.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCopying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a cultural-exchange evaluation of two published cases using a respect checklist; mark Community verification needs honestly.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Geography","learning_intention":"Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate. Analyse published examples of exchange (food, language learning, arts collaborations) and check for stereotypes. First Nations cultural materials require protocols—use only approved sources; do not treat culture as costume.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["GE4-1"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a cultural-exchange evaluation of two published cases using a respect checklist; mark Community verification needs honestly. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using cultural exchange on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.. Correction: Strong answer: Respectful example credits people;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating cultural exchange on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with cultural exchange, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about cultural exchange with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for cultural exchange. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for cultural exchange. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Respectful example credits people;"],"question":"Give one respectful exchange example and one stereotype to avoid, with a brief why. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for cultural exchange; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate.","Respectful example credits people;","Careful use of respect with clear evidence.","Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named respect, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply cultural exchange to new material and name the respect used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","Name respect only, without linking it to cultural exchange."],"question":"Which response best applies cultural exchange with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on cultural exchange. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","Respectful example credits people;"],"question":"Which response best evaluates sources, places or historical/geographic evidence when analysing cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about cultural exchange.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Example: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to","Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.","Name respect only, with no linked example.","Write a cultural-exchange evaluation of two published cases using a respect checklist;","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies cultural exchange to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about cultural exchange with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating cultural exchange on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using cultural exchange on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for cultural exchange. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with cultural exchange, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance.. Correction: Strong answer: Respectful example credits people;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-hsie-y7-w33-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise cultural exchange.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Geography","success_criteria":["I can use cultural exchange with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify respect and explain why it matters.","I can practise stereotype check independently on a new prompt.","I can notice published example needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to critique). Checklist: Is the culture represented by its own members? Is permission/credit clear?\n\nTRY FIRST\nGive one respectful exchange example and one stereotype to avoid, with a brief why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Respectful example credits people; stereotype named without repeating harmful detail.","explain":"Cultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate. Analyse published examples of exchange (food, language learning, arts collaborations) and check for stereotypes. First Nations cultural materials require protocols—use only approved sources; do not treat culture as costume.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Cultural exchange.","caption":"Credit the source.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Locate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">time/place</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">published source</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to criti Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to criti Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to critique). Checklist: Is the culture represented by its own members? Is permission/credit clear? Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Fair claims.","caption":"No invented sacred content.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Perspective</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">whose view</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Limit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what is unknown</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCopying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance. Here is a more productive model: Example: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to critique). Checklist: Is the culture represented by its own members? Is permission/credit clear?\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Respectful example credits people; stereotype named without repeating harmful detail.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Copying First Nations designs or ceremonies for a ''multicultural day'' without guidance. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cultural exchange?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCultural exchange through travel and media can build understanding when it is respectful and accurate. Analyse published examples of exchange (food, language learning, arts collaborations) and check for stereotypes. First Nations cultural materials require protocols—use only approved sources; do not treat culture as costume. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 33, Lesson 2: Cultural exchange","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Interconnections and trade","week":33,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: A sister-school language exchange (published school report) vs a tourist show that mocks sacred dance (to critique). Checklist: Is the culture represented by its own members? Is permission/credit clear?\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Cultural exchange.","caption":"Credit the source.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height